1
00:00:02,840 --> 00:00:27,890
Old art is limited. Not as limited as you think, because a good many old masterpieces are being produced each year. But it's still limited. And if you can wait 100 years, or 60, the other thing, amazingly, that turns out to be inflation-proof is knowledge.

2
00:00:27,890 --> 00:00:40,360
You know, two years after the German inflation was over, German small business still hadn't recovered, and most of the large companies never recovered in that sense until the 50s.

3
00:00:40,360 --> 00:00:57,020
But the physician, the lawyer, the university professor, the accountant had restored their standard of living, restored their capacity to produce and to contribute as if nothing had happened.

4
00:00:57,020 --> 00:01:01,020
And that's been true in the French Revolution just as much.

5
00:01:01,020 --> 00:01:06,020
So that's the only inflation-proof investment I know.

6
00:01:06,020 --> 00:01:14,020
And when you plot savings rates and you say that savings is not capital formation,

7
00:01:14,020 --> 00:01:22,390
you accept the fact that Say's Law has been repealed, but you also accept what Keynes never did,

8
00:01:22,390 --> 00:01:28,690
is that if you repeal it, it is just as possible that there's under-savings as there is that

9
00:01:28,690 --> 00:01:29,670
there's over-savings.

10
00:01:30,360 --> 00:01:35,560
The one theoretical flaw in the Keynesian system who assumed it can only go one way.

11
00:01:36,080 --> 00:01:38,540
If there is no law, it can go either way, you know.

12
00:01:39,020 --> 00:01:47,020
If you don't assume that, it has to be either savings equals investment or there's over-savings.

13
00:01:47,020 --> 00:01:51,000
If you see there is no necessary correlation between the two,

14
00:01:51,770 --> 00:01:56,290
except that you can't have capital formation investment without savings,

15
00:01:57,170 --> 00:02:01,630
then you find that if you take what people consider savings,

16
00:02:02,170 --> 00:02:05,130
which is deferred consumption,

17
00:02:08,140 --> 00:02:16,520
you have about the same rates with one and only one independent variable,

18
00:02:16,900 --> 00:02:19,200
which is the age structure of the society.

19
00:02:19,200 --> 00:02:21,220
It makes sense, you know.

20
00:02:21,220 --> 00:02:28,130
And since all developed societies have roughly the same age structure, roughly,

21
00:02:28,570 --> 00:02:32,830
the Japanese now have the longest age, but that's a year and a half different.

22
00:02:33,170 --> 00:02:38,070
Other than that, we all have the same age structures and the same demographic structures.

23
00:02:38,820 --> 00:02:43,840
You get the same savings rates, they're only collected differently.

24
00:02:44,460 --> 00:02:52,360
But to the extent to which the society is yet organized of taking care of its older people,

25
00:02:52,360 --> 00:03:00,540
which the Japanese are not yet. A larger or greater part of the savings is available for

26
00:03:00,540 --> 00:03:07,430
investment and is not used for transfer payments into consumption. Therefore, one can say with,

27
00:03:07,430 --> 00:03:13,190
I would say, near certainty that within 10 years the Japanese rate of capital formation

28
00:03:13,190 --> 00:03:18,570
will not be appreciably higher than ours, even though their rate of personal savings

29
00:03:18,570 --> 00:03:21,850
may still remain very much higher on paper

30
00:03:21,850 --> 00:03:26,890
because they save in the bank where we save in the Social Security account.

31
00:03:43,120 --> 00:03:45,460
I don't know, I don't think I know it.

32
00:03:46,240 --> 00:06:48,880
Peter Drucker.

33
00:06:49,580 --> 00:06:51,560
Thank you very much, Bob Hawkins.

34
00:06:51,560 --> 00:06:57,180
Let me apologize for not our inability to answer all questions.

35
00:06:57,800 --> 00:07:00,220
We would be here till about midnight

36
00:07:00,220 --> 00:07:05,060
and still not have finished with all of them.

37
00:07:05,260 --> 00:07:20,920
And in a way, I'm very glad that we have to cut it off because there are some I'm very happy I don't have to answer, such as the question from the lady from the New York Times, what kind of an executive will he need tomorrow?

38
00:07:20,920 --> 00:07:31,730
Let me say, let's face it, the kind of people who do have to do the job are exactly the kind of people we have now.

39
00:07:31,730 --> 00:07:35,730
Evolution isn't going to be that fast.

40
00:07:35,730 --> 00:07:43,200
The question is will you be able to think through your policy, your direction,

41
00:07:43,200 --> 00:07:51,360
and will you finally go to work on placing people where their strengths can produce results

42
00:07:51,360 --> 00:07:59,520
results instead of trying to either hope for a different breed, this human being isn't

43
00:07:59,520 --> 00:08:08,920
going to change very much, or making exaggerated expectations in respect to what education

44
00:08:08,920 --> 00:08:10,760
and development can produce.

45
00:08:11,320 --> 00:08:17,920
Believe me, the key to answering the demands of tomorrow is not different people or better

46
00:08:17,920 --> 00:08:26,100
people, which is unlikely to happen, but better place people, where their strengths can really

47
00:08:26,100 --> 00:08:32,760
redound to performance, and it is a neglected area of management is to think through what

48
00:08:32,760 --> 00:08:43,300
Joe Blow or Jane Doe can do, and to make sure he and she is allowed, permitted, expected

49
00:08:43,300 --> 00:08:44,300
to do it.

50
00:08:44,300 --> 00:08:52,560
So I don't believe we are going to see a major change in the human race or a major change in human qualifications.

51
00:08:53,790 --> 00:08:57,150
Neither of them are very rational expectations.

52
00:08:57,330 --> 00:09:07,920
The only way is to improve the yield of what we have, and that is putting people at very specific resources.

53
00:09:08,920 --> 00:09:10,860
No two individuals are alike.

54
00:09:11,520 --> 00:09:19,320
That is to spend a great deal of hard work and thinking on where individuals belong.

55
00:09:19,320 --> 00:09:26,360
And by the way, one question was, what do the Japanese do better than we in using people?

56
00:09:26,700 --> 00:09:37,560
Believe me, since they cannot fire anybody, they are forced to think much harder than we do where you place people.

57
00:09:39,100 --> 00:09:43,460
And in that respect, their immobility is an asset.

58
00:09:43,460 --> 00:09:48,100
and we are not using our mobility sufficiently as an asset

59
00:09:48,100 --> 00:09:52,430
because we are not thinking sufficiently where to place people,

60
00:09:52,430 --> 00:09:55,430
which is already an introduction to my topic,

61
00:09:56,190 --> 00:09:59,410
which is what we might learn from others.

62
00:10:00,030 --> 00:10:03,490
And I quite intentionally picked that topic

63
00:10:03,490 --> 00:10:10,890
because this series began 15 years ago,

64
00:10:11,010 --> 00:10:13,170
I think this is our 16th anniversary,

65
00:10:13,910 --> 00:10:17,430
with one that was caused, or really was a response,

66
00:10:17,910 --> 00:10:21,350
to Savant Schreiber's book on the American Challenge.

67
00:10:22,050 --> 00:10:26,770
We had the first series here, then in 65 or 64, I've forgotten,

68
00:10:27,490 --> 00:10:34,840
in which basically the conviction every place was

69
00:10:34,840 --> 00:10:38,660
that it was at the peak that we had invented management,

70
00:10:38,880 --> 00:10:40,500
which some of you know isn't true,

71
00:10:40,500 --> 00:10:46,180
And we have somehow the secret of management, which, God help us, isn't so easy.

72
00:10:46,660 --> 00:10:53,980
And somehow the others all the world over were organizing themselves very effectively to learn from us.

73
00:10:53,980 --> 00:11:14,660
And to a large extent, you can look upon the development of the post-war period as a diffusion of American management, not technology if you want to call it that, with people who were willing to look at us and say,

74
00:11:14,660 --> 00:11:19,100
what are these people doing that explains their effectiveness

75
00:11:19,100 --> 00:11:23,780
and in what way can we use

76
00:11:23,780 --> 00:11:27,720
it ourselves. The ones who tried

77
00:11:27,720 --> 00:11:31,450
to imitate us didn't get very far.

78
00:11:32,500 --> 00:11:36,820
Let me say that one of the weaknesses of my friends in Great

79
00:11:36,820 --> 00:11:40,980
Britain, in British industry, is that they are too Americanized

80
00:11:40,980 --> 00:11:44,820
in the sense that they are using things

81
00:11:44,820 --> 00:11:48,980
that came from this country, the old remark about two countries

82
00:11:48,980 --> 00:11:50,840
divided by a common language

83
00:11:50,840 --> 00:11:57,100
and simply slapped things on and they don't

84
00:11:57,100 --> 00:12:00,640
fit either their objective reality

85
00:12:00,640 --> 00:12:04,900
or their social and cultural tradition in many cases

86
00:12:04,900 --> 00:12:09,160
and so one of the things that is very clear

87
00:12:09,160 --> 00:12:12,420
is that where you see success in the United Kingdom

88
00:12:12,420 --> 00:12:19,160
It is very heavily built around people who were trained in and by American companies,

89
00:12:20,020 --> 00:12:23,080
who, however, then when they moved into a British company,

90
00:12:23,600 --> 00:12:30,200
thought how to adapt it, what fits and what doesn't, rather than copy it.

91
00:12:30,520 --> 00:12:34,940
And the great majority copied, and copies are weak.

92
00:12:36,440 --> 00:12:40,560
Carbon copies are weak, and they don't really become organic.

93
00:12:40,560 --> 00:12:52,300
And where you see results in these people who learned just the same thing, but did not copy, but then say, what does it mean for us and for me?

94
00:12:52,300 --> 00:13:20,250
And I'm going to talk about some things one can now learn from others who in the last 15 years have been relatively, and in some cases absolutely, more successful and more effective than we have been, but not to imitate, but to say how do we then make this part of our culture, our tradition, our structure, our policies.

95
00:13:20,250 --> 00:13:40,020
And if yesterday some of you were here, yesterday it was many, and you may remember Irving Kristol and you and I had a discussion about leadership and where we talked about the fact that in countries like especially Japan and Germany,

96
00:13:40,020 --> 00:14:00,760
The large, the major business leaders see themselves as responsible for thinking through what the right national policy would be in areas in which business leaders might be expected to have competence.

97
00:14:00,760 --> 00:14:27,120
And I cited the example of the visit I had a few months ago from a Japanese group, the chief executive officers of the 25 largest Japanese companies who came over here, to always have one question, what policy should we in Japan have in respect to retirement, wages, what have you, in view of our changing demographics?

98
00:14:27,920 --> 00:14:32,900
And when I said, now, what do you think is the right thing for business to do,

99
00:14:32,980 --> 00:14:38,760
I was sharply called to order by the chairman who said, that is the next question.

100
00:14:39,160 --> 00:14:42,420
The first question is, what's the right policy for Japan?

101
00:14:43,260 --> 00:14:49,860
Only when we know that do we even think about what the right policy is for our company.

102
00:14:49,860 --> 00:14:54,680
And that is a very different view of the role.

103
00:14:54,680 --> 00:15:17,800
And if you want to see where does it come from, it's root, like so much that is good, is in pure vice. It is basically in vanity in Japan and in Germany, where the businessman was basically not respected in what were, in both countries, bureaucratic traditions.

104
00:15:17,800 --> 00:15:27,400
You have an exaggerated, perhaps, desire on the part of the business leaders to be respectable

105
00:15:27,400 --> 00:15:34,780
and to be gentlemen, and to remove from that stigma of filthy lucre, and so they became

106
00:15:34,780 --> 00:15:37,680
pompous and statesmen.

107
00:15:37,680 --> 00:15:44,230
But it had also its desirable side effects.

108
00:15:44,230 --> 00:15:53,000
phenomenon is purely bad, the side effects are quite good. And one of

109
00:15:53,000 --> 00:15:59,300
them is that they realize how visible they are in today's world, and that one

110
00:15:59,300 --> 00:16:06,360
cannot be that visible without representing. And one of the things I

111
00:16:06,360 --> 00:16:12,900
think one has to learn is that in this world in which the old tradition, for

112
00:16:12,900 --> 00:16:18,840
better or worse of complete separation of the political and the economic no

113
00:16:18,840 --> 00:16:27,450
longer works and sometimes I wish the other Friedman Milton could prevail and

114
00:16:27,450 --> 00:16:37,810
we could restore that separation but I don't think one can restore virginity

115
00:16:37,810 --> 00:16:41,050
and this would be akin to it

116
00:16:41,050 --> 00:16:43,470
and I don't believe it works

117
00:16:43,470 --> 00:16:48,770
and so we have to accept that those two

118
00:16:48,770 --> 00:16:50,490
if they ever were that clean

119
00:16:50,490 --> 00:16:53,650
separated outside of the lecture halls

120
00:16:53,650 --> 00:16:55,430
of the University of Chicago

121
00:16:55,430 --> 00:16:56,590
and I have my doubts

122
00:16:56,590 --> 00:17:00,530
will never be separated again

123
00:17:00,530 --> 00:17:02,710
and then one has to say

124
00:17:02,710 --> 00:17:06,710
the heads of the large institutions

125
00:17:06,710 --> 00:17:14,330
not just businesses, are so visible. They are the only leadership group we have, groups we have in society.

126
00:17:14,810 --> 00:17:24,270
If they do not give a clear trumpet call, then there is no trumpet in the land and only an uncertain sound.

127
00:17:24,970 --> 00:17:33,770
And so that one can learn. And certainly one of the reasons for the performance of, let's say, Germany, Japan,

128
00:17:33,770 --> 00:17:42,030
has been the fact that in those countries the leadership group, not just in business,

129
00:17:42,330 --> 00:17:46,510
but above all in business, sees itself as representative

130
00:17:46,510 --> 00:17:57,710
and as being responsible for thinking through major policy decisions in areas of their competence.

131
00:17:57,710 --> 00:18:12,810
It is very hard in Germany to say whether the Bundesbank and the Ministry of Finance think through policies or the large German commercial banks, because they don't see themselves as separate in that sense.

132
00:18:13,610 --> 00:18:17,650
They say, who in our group knows most about this?

133
00:18:18,710 --> 00:18:24,890
And he may work for the Western Bank or for the Federal Bank, but he's one of us.

134
00:18:24,890 --> 00:18:28,320
And that is a very important difference.

135
00:18:29,340 --> 00:18:35,680
And you cannot import it into a system that, like our system, is based on checks and balances

136
00:18:35,680 --> 00:18:47,250
and on very careful, long-length relationships, and still how does it fit us?

137
00:18:47,670 --> 00:18:55,450
Believe me, I think we have reached the limits of the purely adversary process if we haven't exceeded it.

138
00:18:56,310 --> 00:19:04,570
And sometimes I feel that there's nothing wrong with a republic that's closing its law schools for 10 years,

139
00:19:04,570 --> 00:19:12,140
wouldn't cure, but it's not going to happen. But we have reached the limits of what one

140
00:19:12,140 --> 00:19:22,980
can hope to achieve by purely adversary processes. There is need for integrating leadership again.

141
00:19:22,980 --> 00:19:29,490
The second thing, let me say, is when you look at the economies that have done best,

142
00:19:29,490 --> 00:19:38,110
It is not the economies that, as all Keynesian dominated ones have been doing, started out

143
00:19:38,110 --> 00:19:41,390
with a domestic economy.

144
00:19:41,390 --> 00:19:46,370
Let me say, you all believe that there is a great deal of difference between Keynes

145
00:19:46,370 --> 00:19:53,190
and Milton Friedman, and if you permit my saying so, there is none.

146
00:19:53,190 --> 00:19:55,970
Both are macroeconomists.

147
00:19:55,970 --> 00:20:02,820
Both believe that the economist philosopher can control.

148
00:20:02,820 --> 00:20:11,700
both believe in demand economics and both believe, above all, in the supremacy of the

149
00:20:11,700 --> 00:20:14,780
domestic economy.

150
00:20:14,780 --> 00:20:21,330
All of these, I submit, have been disproven by the events of the last 30 years.

151
00:20:21,330 --> 00:20:26,530
And I think Friedman is a little more obsolete than the Keynesians because he's a little

152
00:20:26,530 --> 00:20:27,530
more doctrinaire.

153
00:20:27,530 --> 00:20:55,510
But that's a bland, that's a rather, I know some Canadians who can compete, and not, but, so, and believe me, if you look at the successful ones, these, by sheer accident, in one case, the Germans, and because of Irving Friedman and the others, the Japanese, did not start out this domestic economy.

154
00:20:55,510 --> 00:21:04,070
You see, very few people realize that the father of the Japanese miracle has the most un-Japanese name of Irving Friedman,

155
00:21:04,350 --> 00:21:10,930
and who in 1950 told the Japanese not to listen to the American economists,

156
00:21:11,730 --> 00:21:18,120
but to practically start out with the world economy rather than the domestic one.

157
00:21:18,120 --> 00:21:33,410
And the successful businesses as well as the successful economies of the last 30 years have accepted the fact that there is a world economy.

158
00:21:33,410 --> 00:21:36,410
It's a dreadfully complicated one.

159
00:21:36,410 --> 00:21:43,410
Irving said we have one currency, the euro dollar, but that's really saying we ain't got no currency, you know.

160
00:21:43,410 --> 00:22:04,840
In a way, we have, because the euro, not euro-dollar, as the standard of value is not terribly satisfactory just now, and nobody controls it and everybody tries to, and it's a muddle.

161
00:22:04,840 --> 00:22:28,260
But still, there is a world economy with its own dynamics and the dynamics lie in the world economy and bluntly, practically all domestic national policies of the last 20 years have been disastrous failures and only where the cue was taken from the world economy have you seen results.

162
00:22:28,260 --> 00:22:38,640
And the lesson to learn from others is that one starts out with the world economy, even if one is primarily domestic.

163
00:22:39,200 --> 00:22:47,060
Very few people know that next to the United States, the Japanese are least dependent on the world economy.

164
00:22:47,760 --> 00:22:54,420
No more than one-tenth of their gross national product comes out of their foreign transactions.

165
00:22:54,420 --> 00:23:01,970
90% is domestic, and only the United States is less dependent.

166
00:23:02,390 --> 00:23:06,170
Every European country is far more dependent on the world economy,

167
00:23:06,650 --> 00:23:12,790
and yet the Japanese, again out of a mixture of intelligence and superstition,

168
00:23:14,730 --> 00:23:21,230
have always seen themselves as totally dependent on the world economy,

169
00:23:21,230 --> 00:23:26,570
which they are not, but as a result have been steered by the world economy

170
00:23:26,570 --> 00:23:31,210
and sought out with the question, what's going on outside Japan,

171
00:23:31,730 --> 00:23:35,910
and not with the question, what's going on inside.

172
00:23:36,570 --> 00:23:41,680
Even though they are just as conscious of the next election,

173
00:23:42,540 --> 00:23:46,840
even though every Japanese electoral district is a swing district,

174
00:23:46,840 --> 00:23:50,080
though they are not hampered as much as we are

175
00:23:50,080 --> 00:23:53,720
by laws forbidding bribery in politics

176
00:23:53,720 --> 00:23:56,900
so they can occasionally still make policy

177
00:23:56,900 --> 00:24:00,500
and then buy support for it

178
00:24:00,500 --> 00:24:04,260
which may not be a bad idea

179
00:24:04,260 --> 00:24:07,300
and at least it works occasionally

180
00:24:07,300 --> 00:24:09,940
but they start out with the question

181
00:24:09,940 --> 00:24:11,360
what goes on outside

182
00:24:11,360 --> 00:24:16,320
and that is something over which we have no control

183
00:24:16,320 --> 00:24:23,860
and that we therefore have to use as an opportunity or as something to adapt to.

184
00:24:23,860 --> 00:24:30,400
And I think increasingly we will have to learn this even though our market is so

185
00:24:30,400 --> 00:24:36,700
much larger, twice the Japanese at least, and even though the country is so much

186
00:24:36,700 --> 00:24:45,160
larger, about in territory maybe 15 times the size of Japan, and even though we are

187
00:24:45,160 --> 00:24:53,020
so heterogeneous as against the extreme homogeneity of Japan. We will still, I think, increasingly

188
00:24:53,020 --> 00:24:59,820
have to learn to understand a very complex and very fast-moving world economy.

189
00:24:59,820 --> 00:25:12,540
The third thing to learn is what marketing means. You know, we invented the term, and I know by now

190
00:25:12,540 --> 00:25:19,610
no company that doesn't preach it, but I'm not sure that I know many that practice it. Marketing

191
00:25:19,610 --> 00:25:28,390
is above all a structural concept. It is thinking through the structure of the outside. And

192
00:25:28,390 --> 00:25:35,550
now it's becoming popular in this country, the Boston Consulting Group concept, to say

193
00:25:35,550 --> 00:25:43,530
you have to have market leadership. It gives you disproportionate profitability. Ladies

194
00:25:43,530 --> 00:25:50,520
and gentlemen, the evidence does not support that theory. The evidence supports that that

195
00:25:50,520 --> 00:25:57,340
market position that fits the market gives you disproportionate returns. But that isn't

196
00:25:57,340 --> 00:26:04,340
leadership necessarily. That's leadership, yes, when the automobile industry of the world

197
00:26:04,340 --> 00:26:12,110
somehow, and we don't know why, turned from a series of national markets into a world

198
00:26:12,110 --> 00:26:19,190
market, then the Chrysler strategy became a strategy for losing, and this isn't hindsight.

199
00:26:19,190 --> 00:26:31,310
Some of us who know that industry said so in 1960, the strategy of being a number three in the national market and satisfying the rest of the world.

200
00:26:31,550 --> 00:26:40,330
Then one had to be one of probably no more than four or five world-class companies or a true specialist.

201
00:26:40,330 --> 00:26:45,510
There was Royce, a checker cab, a jeep, what have you.

202
00:26:45,810 --> 00:26:48,410
And in between, there was only wasteland.

203
00:26:48,410 --> 00:26:49,810
and that

204
00:26:49,810 --> 00:26:52,710
but when you're a book publisher

205
00:26:52,710 --> 00:26:54,990
you know that's a meaningless strategy

206
00:26:54,990 --> 00:26:56,790
if only because of the

207
00:26:56,790 --> 00:26:58,890
language barrier there isn't going to be

208
00:26:58,890 --> 00:26:59,790
world publishing

209
00:26:59,790 --> 00:27:03,090
how many of you

210
00:27:03,090 --> 00:27:04,950
are ever going to be able to

211
00:27:04,950 --> 00:27:07,050
read Chinese I won't I know that

212
00:27:07,050 --> 00:27:09,070
and

213
00:27:09,070 --> 00:27:11,110
secondly the book

214
00:27:11,110 --> 00:27:13,030
in book publishing is a very

215
00:27:13,030 --> 00:27:15,070
